{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"caption\":\"United States v. Griffith\",\"court\":\"D.C. Cir.\",\"date\":\"2017-08-18\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"867 F.3d 1265; No. 15-3061\",\"proposition\":\"A warrant authorizing seizure of every electronic device in a residence lacked probable-cause nexus where officers had no evidence the suspect owned such devices and the warrant exposed co-occupants' devices to search; recent digital-warrant decisions treat Griffith as the principal device-nexus benchmark.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"issuance > probable-cause nexus > digital devices\",\"discovery_path\":\"Recent digital-particularity synthesis and Armendariz line -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/24-1201/24-1201-2026-03-12.html\",\"confidence\":0.91}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Morton\",\"court\":\"5th Cir. en banc\",\"date\":\"2022-08-16\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"46 F.4th 331\",\"proposition\":\"The en banc court resolved a category-spanning cellphone-warrant challenge through Leon good faith rather than adopting the vacated panel's category-specific probable-cause restrictions; recent decisions continue to use Morton as the Fifth Circuit's digital-warrant good-faith baseline.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"particularity > cellphone data categories > good faith\",\"discovery_path\":\"Recent bare-bones-warrant treatment citing Morton -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/25-30105/25-30105-2025-08-29.html\",\"confidence\":0.9}\n{\"caption\":\"Lindell v. United States\",\"court\":\"8th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2023\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"82 F.4th 614\",\"proposition\":\"Rule 41(e)(2)(B) permits a two-step electronic search in which data is first copied or disclosed and responsive evidence is later identified; the presence of extensive unrelated information does not itself turn a crime-limited digital warrant into a general warrant.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"execution > Rule 41(e)(2)(B) > two-step digital search\",\"discovery_path\":\"Recent Eighth Circuit reliance in Franklin -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca8/23-3118/23-3118-2026-06-01.html\",\"confidence\":0.9}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Ivey\",\"court\":\"8th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"91 F.4th 915\",\"proposition\":\"A warrant permitting officers to search anywhere in a cellphone for evidence of identified firearms offenses was sufficiently particular because evidence could be located anywhere on the device and particularity demands practical accuracy, not hypertechnical file or application restrictions.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"particularity > digital devices > whole-device search\",\"discovery_path\":\"Whole-phone particularity thread; collected and applied in Tompkins -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca2/22-599/22-599-2024-09-23.html\",\"confidence\":0.92}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Whipple\",\"court\":\"6th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-02-08\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"92 F.4th 605; No. 23-5126\",\"proposition\":\"Rule 41(e)(2)(B)'s execution deadline governs seizure or on-site copying of a device, not later off-site extraction and review; an eight-month pandemic-era delay in examining a lawfully seized phone did not invalidate the search.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"execution > Rule 41(e)(2)(B) > post-deadline forensic review\",\"discovery_path\":\"Rule 41 digital-execution query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca6/23-5126/23-5126-2024-02-08.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Zelaya-Veliz\",\"court\":\"4th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-02-16\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"94 F.4th 321; No. 22-4656\",\"proposition\":\"Rule 41's two-step process may require Facebook to disclose extensive account data while limiting government seizure to evidence of enumerated crimes. Temporal limits remain important; complex, ongoing conspiracies justify breadth, while a total temporal omission may require reliance on good faith.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"particularity > social-media warrants > subject-matter and temporal limits\",\"discovery_path\":\"Social-media warrant particularity query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/22-4656/22-4656-2024-02-16.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Payne\",\"court\":\"9th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-04-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"99 F.4th 495; No. 22-50262\",\"proposition\":\"Police did not violate the Fifth Amendment by physically using a parolee's thumb to unlock his phone because the act required no cognitive exertion and was non-testimonial. The court expressly reserved situations in which the subject must identify or select the unlocking finger.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"execution > compelled device unlocking > Fifth Amendment\",\"discovery_path\":\"Biometric-unlock warrant thread -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/22-50262/22-50262-2024-04-17.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Sanders\",\"court\":\"6th Cir. en banc\",\"date\":\"2024-06-28\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"106 F.4th 455; No. 21-5945\",\"proposition\":\"Two controlled buys, including surveillance showing the suspect leave the apartment immediately before a buy and return afterward, established the required nexus to search the apartment. Separate writings disputed the majority's additional discussion of inferences applicable to known drug dealers' homes.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"issuance > probable-cause nexus > residence\",\"discovery_path\":\"En-banc warrant-nexus query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca6/21-5945/21-5945-2024-06-28.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Salas\",\"court\":\"10th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-07-03\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"106 F.4th 1050; No. 23-8027\",\"proposition\":\"A drug-evidence warrant sufficiently particularized the authorized forensic search of an iPhone rather than merely its seizure; discovery of child-sexual-abuse material during a reasonable device search did not make the warrant an impermissible general warrant.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"particularity > cellphone warrants > search authorization and scope\",\"discovery_path\":\"Digital-warrant scope query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/23-8027/23-8027-2024-07-03.html\",\"confidence\":0.98}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie\",\"court\":\"4th Cir. panel\",\"date\":\"2024-07-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"107 F.4th 319; No. 22-4489\",\"proposition\":\"The panel held that obtaining two hours of Google Location History was not a Fourth Amendment search because the user voluntarily exposed the data to Google. Rehearing en banc vacated the panel opinion, and the Supreme Court later rejected its search holding.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"geofence warrants > reasonable expectation of privacy > superseded panel rule\",\"discovery_path\":\"Geofence circuit-split query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/22-4489/22-4489-2024-07-09.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Davis\",\"court\":\"11th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-07-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-10184\",\"proposition\":\"A defendant lacked Fourth Amendment standing to challenge a geofence return associated with his girlfriend's Google account and data; the court therefore did not decide the warrant's substantive constitutionality.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"geofence warrants > standing > third-party account data\",\"discovery_path\":\"Geofence circuit-position query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca11/23-10184/23-10184-2024-07-30.html\",\"confidence\":0.98}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Smith\",\"court\":\"5th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-08-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"110 F.4th 817\",\"proposition\":\"Geofence warrants are general warrants categorically prohibited by the Fourth Amendment because they search everyone within a geographic area without individualized probable cause; suppression was nevertheless denied under Leon because officers acted before governing precedent existed.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"geofence warrants > particularity > categorical general-warrant rule\",\"discovery_path\":\"Geofence circuit-split query -> https://caselaw.findlaw.com/court/us-5th-circuit/116547850.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"Cuervo v. Sorenson\",\"court\":\"10th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-08-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-1387\",\"proposition\":\"General concerns about unknown occupants, possible firearms, and officer safety did not create case-specific exigency excusing knock-and-announce before a forceful entry involving chemical munitions; the alleged violation was sufficiently clearly established to defeat qualified immunity.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"execution > knock-and-announce > exigency and tactical entry\",\"discovery_path\":\"Knock-and-announce published-circuit query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/22-1387/22-1387-2024-08-30.html\",\"confidence\":0.97}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Tompkins\",\"court\":\"2d Cir.\",\"date\":\"2024-09-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"118 F.4th 280; Nos. 22-599, 22-602\",\"proposition\":\"A warrant authorizing forensic examination of a cellphone and associated electronic storage encompassed an SD card inserted in the phone; practical accuracy, not technical component-by-component enumeration, controls digital-warrant scope.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"execution > warrant scope > component storage media\",\"discovery_path\":\"Digital-component warrant query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca2/22-599/22-599-2024-09-23.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Brown\",\"court\":\"D.C. Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-01-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-3074 et al.\",\"proposition\":\"Compelling a suspect to select and use the finger that unlocks his cellphone was testimonial because the act communicated knowledge, access, and control. Evidence and later-warrant fruits derived from the act required suppression; the warrant expressly withheld authority to compel unlocking, so good faith could not excuse the execution.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"execution > compelled device unlocking > Fifth Amendment testimonial act\",\"discovery_path\":\"Biometric-unlock split query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/cadc/23-3074/23-3074-2025-01-17.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Williams\",\"court\":\"4th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-03-04\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"Nos. 23-4568, 23-4595\",\"proposition\":\"Assuming officers entered without knocking and announcing, specific exigencies—including the target's firearm history, violent-gang affiliation, methamphetamine activity, and threats—justified the no-knock entry. The court did not decide whether suppression remains available for a standalone 18 U.S.C. § 3109 violation after Hudson.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"execution > knock-and-announce > case-specific exigency\",\"discovery_path\":\"Knock-and-announce published-circuit query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/23-4568/23-4568-2025-03-04.html\",\"confidence\":0.98}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Santiago\",\"court\":\"10th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-04-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"135 F.4th 1235; No. 24-6015\",\"proposition\":\"A state digital warrant was an overbroad general warrant, and its defects were too obvious for objectively reasonable reliance. Evidence from that search had to be excised from the later federal affidavit; without it, the federal warrant lacked probable cause and suppression followed.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"particularity > general warrants > tainted successive warrant\",\"discovery_path\":\"Digital general-warrant and taint query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/24-6015/24-6015-2025-04-30.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie\",\"court\":\"4th Cir. en banc\",\"date\":\"2025-04-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"136 F.4th 100; No. 22-4489\",\"proposition\":\"The en banc court affirmed in a one-sentence per curiam disposition and divided 7-7 over whether acquiring Google Location History was a search; most judges finding a search also viewed the warrant as defective but would apply good faith. The Supreme Court later vacated this judgment.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"geofence warrants > en banc disposition > no precedential majority\",\"discovery_path\":\"En-banc geofence query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/22-4489/22-4489-2025-04-30.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Whiteside\",\"court\":\"6th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-05-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1173\",\"proposition\":\"A warrant can be constitutionally issued even when the judge does not sign the warrant form, provided clear contemporaneous objective evidence shows that the judge made the probable-cause determination and authorized the search; the Sixth Circuit joined the other circuits rejecting a per se signature rule.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"issuance > judicial authorization > unsigned warrant\",\"discovery_path\":\"Unsigned-warrant issuance query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca6/24-1173/24-1173-2025-05-19.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Ray\",\"court\":\"4th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-06-03\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"141 F.4th 129; No. 24-4024\",\"proposition\":\"A military search authorization permitted seizure of a cellphone but not a search of its contents. Agents exceeded the authorization's unambiguous scope, and Leon could not convert authority to seize into authority to search.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"execution > warrant scope > seizure versus search\",\"discovery_path\":\"Execution-scope query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/24-4024/24-4024-2025-06-03.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Krueger\",\"court\":\"4th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-07-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-4328\",\"proposition\":\"Eleven-month-old BitTorrent evidence was not stale because repeated downloading supported the collector inference and forensic remnants can persist after deletion. A later federal-warrant delay did not prolong the defendant's dispossession because the devices remained independently in state custody.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"staleness > persistent digital evidence; execution > possessory delay\",\"discovery_path\":\"Digital-staleness and delayed-warrant query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/24-4328/24-4328-2025-07-25.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. McCarley-Connin\",\"court\":\"6th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-08-22\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-3055\",\"proposition\":\"Probable-cause review of a warrant is confined to sworn material presented to the issuing magistrate. A defendant may use extrinsic evidence to attack an otherwise facially sufficient affidavit only after satisfying Franks; Florida v. Harris created no separate evidentiary-hearing route for challenging a detection dog's reliability after a warrant issued.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"issuance > neutral magistrate > four-corners and Franks review\",\"discovery_path\":\"Neutral-magistrate and four-corners query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca6/24-3055/24-3055-2025-08-22.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Wilson\",\"court\":\"5th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025-08-29\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-30105\",\"proposition\":\"An affidavit describing a public firearm offense but offering no factual bridge to the suspect's girlfriend's apartment was bare bones. Probable cause to arrest did not supply probable cause to search the residence, and Leon could not save the conjectural nexus; suppression was affirmed over dissent.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"issuance > probable-cause nexus > residence and firearm\",\"discovery_path\":\"Bare-bones residential-nexus query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/25-30105/25-30105-2025-08-29.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Hernandez\",\"court\":\"11th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-02-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-10482\",\"proposition\":\"Rule 41(e)(2)(B) permits off-site extraction and review of electronic information after the warrant's nominal deadline when the device was timely seized or copied. Constitutional probable cause must still exist at execution, but preservation of the phone meant the later extraction did not make the information stale; the Eleventh Circuit joined the prevailing circuit rule.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"execution > Rule 41(e)(2)(B) > later extraction and staleness\",\"discovery_path\":\"Post-deadline digital-extraction query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca11/24-10482/24-10482-2026-02-19.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"Armendariz v. City of Colorado Springs\",\"court\":\"10th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-03-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1201\",\"proposition\":\"Plaintiffs plausibly alleged clearly established overbreadth and nexus violations in warrants authorizing seizure of numerous devices, sweeping device-data and political-keyword searches, and all Facebook posts, chats, and events. A keyword search is itself subject to particularity, vague relevance language does not constrain discretion, and an affidavit cures a warrant only when expressly incorporated and limiting.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"particularity > digital devices, keyword searches, and social media\",\"discovery_path\":\"Digital overbreadth and keyword-warrant query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/24-1201/24-1201-2026-03-12.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Tew\",\"court\":\"10th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-04-06\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1333\",\"proposition\":\"An Apple-cloud warrant lacked particularity where the detailed affidavit was not attached or incorporated and the warrant relied on broad statutory references and vague conspiracy-related language. Leon still applied because the warrant made a non-glaring effort to limit the account, offenses, dates, and evidence categories.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"particularity > cloud-account warrants > incorporation and good faith\",\"discovery_path\":\"Cloud-warrant particularity query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/24-1333/24-1333-2026-04-06.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Franklin\",\"court\":\"8th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-06-01\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-3118\",\"proposition\":\"An Instagram warrant was sufficiently particular where it identified the account, crimes, responsive information categories, and an investigation-linked time period. Disclosure of unrelated account material did not make it a general warrant, extending Ivey and Lindell to a social-media account.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"particularity > social-media accounts > crime, category, and time limits\",\"discovery_path\":\"Recent social-media-warrant query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca8/23-3118/23-3118-2026-06-01.html\",\"confidence\":0.98}\n{\"caption\":\"Chatrie v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2026-06-29\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"609 U.S. ___; No. 25-112\",\"proposition\":\"Police conduct a Fourth Amendment search when they compel Google to disclose an individual's Location History, even for a short period and despite third-party storage. The Court vacated the Fourth Circuit and remanded for separate probable-cause and particularity analysis at every step of the geofence process.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"geofence warrants > Fourth Amendment search > step-specific warrant requirements\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS geofence query -> https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/609/25-112/\",\"confidence\":1.0}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Gonzalez-Arocho\",\"court\":\"1st Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-07-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-1041\",\"proposition\":\"Agents who knew they had seized an iPhone 13 could not rely on a warrant specifically identifying an iPhone 6s with a different IMEI. Their failure to resolve the discrepancy or obtain a new warrant defeated objective good faith, and suppression was affirmed.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"execution > warrant scope > wrong digital device\",\"discovery_path\":\"Wrong-device execution query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca1/25-1041/25-1041-2026-07-09.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Holcomb\",\"court\":\"9th Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-07-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-469; superseding withdrawn 132 F.4th 1127\",\"proposition\":\"A time-unlimited dominion-and-control clause made a computer warrant overbroad and insufficiently particular. Even assuming good faith at the search's outset, officers had to stop when an early video showed the alleged rape almost certainly had not occurred and dissipated probable cause; later-discovered evidence was suppressed. The superseding opinion replaced the March 2025 opinion's broader outset-good-faith analysis.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"execution > continuing probable cause; particularity > temporal limits\",\"discovery_path\":\"Superseding digital-warrant opinion query -> https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/23-469/23-469-2026-07-17.html\",\"confidence\":0.99}"}}
